@inject IDraftStateService DraftStateService

@*
    What the answers have become: the panel that opens under the entry's own tag on the map.

    It has no heading and no name of its own, because the thing it belongs to is directly above it and
    already lit — the tag is the trigger, this is what the trigger opens and anything here repeating
    the name would make two objects out of one.

    Folded away by default, because these rows are not read but looked up, when the client wonders
    what has been written down about this one so far. Laid open under the question they were a second
    screen of small print beside the one thing they are being asked to do.

    What is still empty is shown faint rather than hidden — a panel that grows a new row every few
    turns reads as a form growing under the client, where one that fills in place reads as their own
    domain taking shape.
*@

@* One row per field a section of the agent can carry — name, description, button, the button's
   request — computed once by AgentRows.Of so this panel and the caret+count on the map's own tag
   can never disagree about what is written down. A row with no value yet is shown faint rather
   than omitted, so the panel reads as the client's own domain filling in rather than a form
   growing new fields under them.

   The Toolkit row is the one exception: its Value is only a stand-in for the empty check and this
   is what actually renders it, one tool per line straight off the agent's own Tools — bolding a
   signature this way is real markup, not a string built and escaped by hand. *@
<div class="rows-open">
    @foreach (AgentRows.Row row in AgentRows.Of(State))
    {
        <div class="drop @(row.Value is null ? "empty" : "") @(row.Moved ? "moved" : "")">
            <span class="what">@row.What</span>
            @if (row.What == "Toolkit" && State.Agent.Tools.Count > 0)
            {
                <div class="value tools">
                    @foreach (ToolDraft tool in State.Agent.Tools)
                    {
                        <div class="tool-line">&bull; <strong>@AgentRows.Signature(tool)</strong>@(string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(tool.Description) ? null : $" — {tool.Description}")</div>
                    }
                </div>
            }
            else
            {
                <span class="value">@(row.Value ?? "not yet")</span>
            }
        </div>
    }
</div>

@*
    What Alembic believes about this counter, under what it has written for it. Shown because a
    memory nobody can see is indistinguishable from an invention: the questions from here on are
    built out of these sentences, so the one person who can tell a wrong one from a right one — the
    client — has to be able to read them. What was read off an uploaded configuration says so, since
    nobody ever said it, and every line can be struck out by the person whose shop it is.
*@
@if (Known.Count > 0)
{
    <div class="known">
        <span class="what">what I understand about your work here</span>

        @foreach (KnownFact fact in Known)
        {
            <div class="fact @(fact.Inferred ? "read" : null)">
                <span class="subject">@fact.Subject</span>
                <span class="said">@fact.Fact</span>
                @if (fact.Inferred)
                {
                    <span class="how">read off your file, not said</span>
                }
                <button class="strike" title="This is not true of my work" @onclick="() => Forget(fact)">&times;</button>
            </div>
        }
    </div>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The interview's live state, holding the agent currently under construction and every field
    /// written into it so far. This component reads it purely — it renders a snapshot and never
    /// mutates it.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public InterviewState State { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>
    /// What is on record about the work at this counter, the business's own facts included.
    /// </summary>
    // Both, because a client reading this is checking what Alembic believes rather than where it
    // filed it: which of two lists a sentence sits in is Alembic's business and correcting a wrong
    // one is theirs.
    private IReadOnlyList<KnownFact> Known =>
        [.. (DraftStateService.Current?.Learned ?? []), .. State.Agent.Known];

    /// <summary>
    /// Strikes out something that is not true of their work, wherever it was filed.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The client's word is final here and nothing asks them to confirm it: what they are striking
    /// is either a reading of their own uploaded file or something taken down wrong, and either way
    /// the person whose shop it is has just said so. Every step after this one opens without it.
    /// </remarks>
    private void Forget(KnownFact fact)
    {
        State.Agent.Known.Remove(fact);
        DraftStateService.Current?.Learned.Remove(fact);
        DraftStateService.Set(DraftStateService.Current);
    }
}
